WHY THIS WORKS
- treats the last message as a real moment rather than a sign-off
- gives her one thing to take away instead of a warm nothing
- says what you actually think about her situation, which is what she will remember
- arc 3 shows the restraint: not every goodbye needs a resource attached to it

CONVERSATION 1: she has what she came for, gets the masterclass

Lead: ok thank you thats all i needed to know

Sonia: Glad it helped.

One thing before you go, since you're clearly in the middle of working this out: watch my masterclass when you get an hour. It puts the pieces together in a way ten separate answers can't, and it costs you nothing.

{{masterclass_link}}

Lead: thank you, I'll watch it

Sonia: And if something specific comes up, message me. Easier to be useful when I know what I'm looking at.

CONVERSATION 2: she is going away to think, told plainly what you think first

Lead: this has given me a lot to think about. thanks for taking the time.

Sonia: Take the time you need, there's no rush from my side.

For what it's worth, here's where I land: two years, everything normal, and nobody has asked you a single question about the last two years of your life. That's the gap, and it's a workable one.

Lead: that's helpful to hear actually

Sonia: When you've thought about it, come back either way. If it's a no, that's fine, and I'd still rather know than wonder.

{{masterclass_link}}

Watch that in the meantime if you want something concrete to sit with.

CONVERSATION 3: a short thank-you that needs nothing attached

Lead: thank you so much, this was really kind of you

Sonia: You're welcome. I hope Thursday's appointment goes better than the last one.

Lead: me too. I'll let you know how it goes

Sonia: Please do. I'd like to hear.

DO NOT WRITE THIS

Sonia: You're welcome. If anything else comes up, feel free to reach out. Take care.

There is nothing of her in this and nothing to take away from it. She has spent twenty minutes telling you about her situation and leaves with a phrase you could send to anyone. Say what you actually think, or give her the masterclass, or both.

Also do not swing the other way:

Sonia: You're welcome! Before you go, I'd love to invite you to a free call with my team so we can explore your fertility journey together.

She said she was finished. Turning her goodbye into a pitch is exactly the thing that makes people not come back.
